/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Each electrode carries the biopotential, a half-cell offset up to a few hundred mV DC, and 50 Hz mains pickup.
   - The wanted part is Vd = V1 − V2.
   - Ad is differential gain (no unit); Acm is common-mode gain (no unit); CMRR is the common-mode rejection ratio as a plain number; CMRR(dB) is the same ratio in decibels. 20 is used because both are amplitude ratios.
   - CMRR matters more than raw gain because the shared part is much bigger.
   - A one-op-amp differential amplifier has input impedance set by its input resistors, often near 10 kΩ, far below electrode impedance.
   - The instrumentation amplifier fixes both with three op-amps.
   - G is differential gain of the input stage (no unit); R is the feedback resistor inside each buffer, in ohms; Rg is the single external gain-setting resistor, in ohms.
   - Input offset voltage is the small DC difference between op-amp inputs, and gain multiplies it.
   - Noise is also multiplied by gain.
   - An isolation barrier (transformer or optocoupler) separates the patient circuit from mains.
*/
