/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A lead is a view: a fixed pair of electrodes with defined polarity, so the same heart looks different from different angles.
   - The three standard limb leads use right arm (RA), left arm (LA) and left leg (LL).
   - RA, LA and LL sit at the corners of an equilateral triangle with the heart at its centre, the Einthoven triangle.
   - The Wilson central terminal, WCT = (RA + LA + LL)/3, is the unipolar reference.
   - The signal path is fixed: electrodes, lead selector, buffer, bio-amplifier, filters, ADC, display.
   - Two scales make the paper readable.
   - time is in s; length is the distance on paper in mm; speed is the paper speed in mm/s (25 or 50); RR is the interval between two QRS complexes in s; heart rate is in beats per minute.
   - The calibration pulse is the machine's honesty check.
   - The trace repeats in a fixed order: P wave (atrial depolarisation), PR segment, QRS complex (ventricular depolarisation), ST segment, T wave (ventricular repolarisation).
   - Vectorcardiography (VCG) records the same dipole but keeps its direction.
*/
