/* book exam traps: Paper speed sets time and calibration sets voltage. A trace can look normal and still be wrong if the 1 mV bar is not 10 mm tall. | Einthoven's law is II = I + III. Reversing signs, or writing I = II + III, is the most common slip. | At 25 mm/s one small square is 0.04 s. Students often use 0.02 s, the value at 50 mm/s, and halve every duration. | aVR is normally negative for P, QRS and T because the heart vector points away from the right arm. A positive aVR is a finding, not a normal variant. | VCG is not a different signal. It uses orthogonal Frank leads X, Y and Z on the same electrical activity, so a normal ECG and a normal VCG must agree. */
